WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.056
Picture a shared office folder, with five files in it.

2
00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:08.709
Your manager asks a simple question.

3
00:00:08.709 --> 00:00:11.762
Which one did we send to the shop on Tuesday?

4
00:00:11.762 --> 00:00:12.644
Nobody knows.

5
00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:14.545
Notice how the names go.

6
00:00:14.545 --> 00:00:17.273
Final, then final two, then final real.

7
00:00:17.273 --> 00:00:20.631
File names start out honest, and end up hopeful.

8
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:22.680
So the point really is this.

9
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.660
A name can't remember history.

10
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.507
We need something that does.

11
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:31.092
Git is the tool that keeps every saved version of a project for you.

12
00:00:31.092 --> 00:00:32.630
Picture an office again.

13
00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:39.617
A desk for the pages you're working on, a tray for the pages that are ready, and a filing cabinet behind you.

14
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:42.932
When a page is ready, it goes in the tray.

15
00:00:42.932 --> 00:00:49.243
Whenever you say save, a clerk photographs everything in the tray, and files the photo in the cabinet.

16
00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:59.180
In Git's words, the desk is the working tree, the tray is the staging area, and the cabinet is the repository, a hidden folder called .git.

17
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.787
Each photo is a commit.

18
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.671
Let's open the cabinet.

19
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.218
Each photo is a full snapshot of the project.

20
00:01:06.218 --> 00:01:11.675
And it's named by a hash of its content, a short fingerprint like d6aad6e.

21
00:01:11.675 --> 00:01:14.845
Same content, same name, on every computer.

22
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:18.636
Each photo also remembers the one before it, its parent.

23
00:01:18.636 --> 00:01:22.013
So history is a chain, and you can walk it backwards.

24
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:26.090
Now, which photo is the newest on the main line of work?

25
00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.775
Git keeps that answer on a sticky note.

26
00:01:28.775 --> 00:01:32.631
The note is called main, and it holds one name, d6aad6e.

27
00:01:32.631 --> 00:01:34.697
That note is the whole branch.

28
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:39.828
And HEAD is the you are here arrow, like the one on a map in a shopping centre.

29
00:01:39.828 --> 00:01:40.951
It points at main.

30
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.989
Let's add a line to the README, and commit it.

31
00:01:43.989 --> 00:01:44.970
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.117
What does Git copy when you commit?

33
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.691
Nothing gets copied.

34
00:01:50.691 --> 00:01:56.180
Git files one new photo, 6e176d6, and moves the sticky note up to it.

35
00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.680
HEAD comes along, because it points at main.

36
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.609
Now the mistake.

37
00:02:01.609 --> 00:02:05.465
You meant to undo one commit, and keep its changes.

38
00:02:05.465 --> 00:02:09.017
But your fingers typed git reset --hard HEAD~2.

39
00:02:09.233 --> 00:02:12.550
Git answers, HEAD is now at c711550.

40
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.497
The note slides back two photos.

41
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.562
You run git log, and the top two are gone.

42
00:02:18.562 --> 00:02:20.129
Your commit, and Ana's.

43
00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:26.896
This is the moment people start typing very fast, which is exactly the wrong moment to type anything.

44
00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:29.691
So slow down, and ask what really happened.

45
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.690
Here's the natural guess.

46
00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:37.632
The log is the history, so if a commit is missing from the log, it must be deleted.

47
00:02:37.632 --> 00:02:38.777
Pause and guess.

48
00:02:38.777 --> 00:02:39.207
Is it?

49
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.086
Let's look.

50
00:02:42.086 --> 00:02:46.450
git log starts at the sticky note, and walks back through the parents.

51
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:47.884
It never looks forward.

52
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.494
So the two photos are still in the cabinet.

53
00:02:50.494 --> 00:02:52.999
The note just doesn't point at them any more.

54
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.279
We can even ask for one by name.

55
00:02:56.279 --> 00:03:00.898
git cat-file -t 6e176d6 prints its type, commit.

56
00:03:00.898 --> 00:03:01.860
It exists.

57
00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:03.918
So reset deleted nothing.

58
00:03:03.918 --> 00:03:05.621
It moved a sticky note.

59
00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:09.097
But how do you find a photo whose name you forgot?

60
00:03:09.097 --> 00:03:10.794
The clerk keeps a logbook.

61
00:03:10.794 --> 00:03:14.776
Every time HEAD moves, the logbook gets a line, newest first.

62
00:03:14.776 --> 00:03:15.951
That's git reflog.

63
00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:17.924
Read it from the top.

64
00:03:17.924 --> 00:03:20.183
We reset, moving to HEAD~2.

65
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:24.785
Before that, we committed, Say that prices include VAT.

66
00:03:24.785 --> 00:03:28.801
There's our lost commit, one line down, 6e176d6.

67
00:03:29.833 --> 00:03:33.617
Now that we know its name, there are two ways back.

68
00:03:33.617 --> 00:03:41.111
The gentle one: git branch rescue 6e176d6 puts a second sticky note on that photo, and moves nothing.

69
00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:47.406
Or move main itself back to where it was one step ago, with git reset --hard 'HEAD@{1}'.

70
00:03:47.406 --> 00:03:50.925
HEAD@{1} means where HEAD was before the last move.

71
00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:53.002
HEAD is now at 6e176d6.

72
00:03:53.002 --> 00:03:58.528
The note slides back up, and both photos are on the main line again.

73
00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:01.749
So now you know why a branch is so cheap.

74
00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:03.652
It's a sticky note on a commit.

75
00:04:03.652 --> 00:04:06.229
And most undo commands just move the note.

76
00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:10.147
But every tool has a limit, and here's this one's.

77
00:04:10.147 --> 00:04:12.377
The logbook only knows about commits.

78
00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:15.089
Picture an edit that lived only on your desk.

79
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:20.566
Run git reset --hard, and the desk is wiped to match the last photo.

80
00:04:20.566 --> 00:04:23.587
The reflog has no entry for that draft.

81
00:04:23.587 --> 00:04:25.368
grep counts zero lines.

82
00:04:25.368 --> 00:04:26.143
It's gone.

83
00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:28.766
So, one habit before any hard reset.

84
00:04:28.766 --> 00:04:30.232
Commit or stash first.

85
00:04:30.232 --> 00:04:35.498
A commit you reset away stays recoverable for at least thirty days, by default.

86
00:04:35.498 --> 00:04:38.297
An edit on your desk, not even one second.

87
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:42.516
And if you already pushed a commit, don't move a shared note at all.

88
00:04:42.516 --> 00:04:47.004
git revert adds a new commit that undoes the old one, so history only grows.

89
00:04:48.933 --> 00:04:50.421
The moral of the story.

90
00:04:50.421 --> 00:04:52.554
Anything committed can come back.

91
00:04:52.554 --> 00:04:54.818
Git rarely throws your photos away.

92
00:04:54.818 --> 00:04:57.792
It moves notes, and it writes down every move.

93
00:04:58.600 --> 00:04:59.633
A quick check.

94
00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:01.479
You run git reset --hard.

95
00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:02.881
Which one survives?

96
00:05:02.881 --> 00:05:07.458
A commit you made an hour ago, or an edit you never committed?

97
00:05:07.458 --> 00:05:08.639
Pause and guess.

98
00:05:10.833 --> 00:05:11.596
The commit.

99
00:05:11.596 --> 00:05:13.398
The reflog still lists it.

100
00:05:13.398 --> 00:05:16.656
The edit lived only on your desk, so it's gone.
